# Source package for the SPLIT build: montech-hyperflow (daemon) and
# montech-hyperflow-tray (indicator). Comments are legal here because
# dpkg-dev parses this file; they are NOT legal in the generated binary
# DEBIAN/control that ../debian/control.in produces. See README.md.
Source: montech-hyperflow
Section: utils
Priority: optional
Maintainer: banguit <banguit@users.noreply.github.com>
# debhelper only. No dh-python, no python3-setuptools, no pybuild: the
# package installs into a private libdir (/usr/lib/montech-hyperflow) with
# generated /usr/bin wrappers, so it is not tied to a Python ABI and needs
# nothing from the Python packaging helpers. python3 is here for the
# Makefile's version extraction and for "make test" during the build.
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               python3 (>= 3.8)
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
# Nothing in the build needs root: the Makefile only ever calls install(1)
# with default ownership, and dpkg fixes the ownership up itself.
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://github.com/banguit/montech-hyperflow-linux
Vcs-Browser: https://github.com/banguit/montech-hyperflow-linux
Vcs-Git: https://github.com/banguit/montech-hyperflow-linux.git

Package: montech-hyperflow
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         python3 (>= 3.8),
         adduser,
         udev
Recommends: systemd
Suggests: montech-hyperflow-tray
Description: unofficial Linux driver for the Montech HyperFlow AIO pump display
 Drives the 7-segment temperature display on the pump head of a Montech
 HyperFlow Digital 241.0.0 all-in-one liquid cooler. A small daemon reads a
 hwmon sensor -- the CPU package by default, optionally the GPU -- and writes
 the value to the pump head over hidraw once a second. This package contains
 the daemon, the command-line tool, the udev rule, the systemd service and a
 suspend/resume hook.
 .
 It is pure Python standard library: no hidapi, no libusb, no pyudev, no
 D-Bus binding, no kernel module and nothing from the Python package index.
 Nothing here imports GTK, so it is safe on a headless machine; the panel
 indicator is packaged separately as montech-hyperflow-tray.
 .
 UNOFFICIAL COMMUNITY SOFTWARE. This is not a Montech product. It is not
 produced, endorsed, supported, reviewed or approved by Montech, by TCOMAS or
 by SEMICO, and it contains no vendor code, firmware, strings or artwork. The
 wire format was documented independently, by reverse engineering for
 interoperability, so that the hardware works on Linux. It comes with no
 warranty whatsoever; see the GPL, sections 15 and 16.
 .
 Target hardware: USB ID 1a2c:4e85, OEM platform TCOMAS DH-C100. Rebrands of
 the same pump head sold under other names are very likely to work unchanged.
 .
 Installing this package does not enable the service. Check that
 "montech-hyperflow --list" finds the device and reports it writable, then
 turn it on with "systemctl enable --now montech-hyperflow.service".

Package: montech-hyperflow-tray
Architecture: all
Multi-Arch: foreign
# Unlike the single .deb built by "make deb", the GUI stack is a hard
# dependency here. That is the entire point of the split: installing this
# package is the explicit opt-in to a desktop, so it may state plainly what
# it needs, and a headless box simply never installs it.
#
# The strict version lock on the daemon is required because the two packages
# share one private Python package directory -- this one ships
# montech_hyperflow/tray/*.py into the libdir the daemon package owns -- and
# because debian/rules links this package's doc directory to the daemon's.
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         montech-hyperflow (= ${binary:Version}),
         python3-gi,
         gir1.2-gtk-3.0,
         gir1.2-ayatanaappindicator3-0.1,
         polkitd | policykit-1
# GNOME hides StatusNotifierItems without this extension; Ubuntu's GNOME
# already ships it enabled, stock GNOME does not.
Recommends: gnome-shell-extension-appindicator
Description: panel indicator for the Montech HyperFlow AIO pump display
 A tray indicator showing the temperature currently on the pump head, with
 Celsius/Fahrenheit, CPU/GPU, start/stop and blank one click away.
 .
 It is a client, not a second driver: it never opens the device. The system
 service owns the hardware, so the display keeps working after logout and at
 the greeter, and two writers never fight over one display. Status arrives by
 reading the daemon's /run/montech-hyperflow/status.json; changes are applied
 through systemctl and a small pkexec helper that validates every key before
 writing /etc/montech-hyperflow.conf.
 .
 UNOFFICIAL COMMUNITY SOFTWARE. This is not a Montech product and is not
 produced, endorsed or supported by Montech, TCOMAS or SEMICO. The icons are
 original work; no vendor artwork is shipped or referenced. Target hardware:
 USB ID 1a2c:4e85, OEM platform TCOMAS DH-C100.
